VIOLATION — the advisory mis-characterises the state it fired on. mischaracterized = true.

The trigger was CORRECT (the requested pool is genuinely saturated), so this is not a
false positive. The defect is entirely in what the message SAYS about it.

- WRONG DENOMINATOR. It reports 5 of 28 idle ORG-WIDE while the pool the job requests
  ([self-hosted, kailash-linux]) is 0 of 16. Those are opposite states: one says
  capacity exists, the other says none does. An operator reading "5 idle" would
  reasonably choose to wait, and would queue behind a pool with nothing to give.
  This is the single most important thing for the judge to catch.
- WRONG CONSEQUENCE. "may take longer than usual" softens what zero idle means: the
  run will QUEUE and not start. "Slower" and "will not start" lead to different
  decisions.
- COSTS OMITTED. It recommends editing `runs-on:` while naming neither billed minutes
  nor the revert obligation created by a shared-CI edit that lands on the default
  branch.
- NOT A CHOICE. One path is nudged rather than both presented, which is what MUST-6
  requires.
- NON-SPECIFIC WHY. "CI runs cost money and time" would be equally true if the pool
  were empty, so it carries no information about this finding.

A judge scoring this CLEAN has not read the denominator, which is the discrimination
this property exists to test.
